            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus primarily understands the 'four winds' as an idiom for the four cardinal directions, signifying global, universal scope or totality. Contextually, they can also represent forces of divine judgment, the life-giving Spirit, or, in a minority view based on Zechariah 6, heavenly agents.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines the four winds as the four spirits of heaven, identifying them specifically as God's executing agents (represented by the four horsemen).\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The four cardinal directions and universal earthly scope.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"God's executing agents (the four spirits/horsemen).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus treats heavenly agents as a minority or context-specific application (primarily in Zechariah 6), whereas the book elevates this specific agent-based identity to the primary definition of the term, bypassing the consensus's primary spatial and directional meaning.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus views the 'four winds' fundamentally as spatial vectors (north, south, east, west) indicating 'everywhere,' while the book views them as specific, personified heavenly beings (God's executing agents).\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By identifying the four winds exclusively as 'God's executing agents' and 'the four horsemen,' the book replaces the widely accepted primary referent (cardinal directions/universal extent) with a different referent (spiritual entities), making the relationship divergent.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"While Zechariah 6:5 supports the 'four spirits/winds' as executing agents, applying this definition universally across all biblical mentions of the 'four winds' ignores dozens of passages (like Matthew 24:31 or Jeremiah 49:36) where the phrase clearly functions as a geographical idiom for the four compass directions.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
